@page "/how-it-works"
@* How Wend Your Way works (user, 2026-10-19): the parts, how they connect and what it's built on,
   for whoever opens the app. Reached from the Help panel. *@

<PageTitle>How Wend Your Way works</PageTitle>

<main class="how">
    <a class="back" href="./">← Back to the map</a>

    <header>
        <h1>How Wend Your Way works</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            A planner for running, gravel-riding and trail-riding Loops, and point-to-point routes, in England and Wales that favour footpaths, alleys and
            cut-throughs over roads with traffic. Everything runs on this laptop; only the map pictures come
            from outside.
        </p>
        <ul class="ground-key" aria-label="Ground, as coloured on the map">
            <li><span class="swatch off-road"></span>Off-road</li>
            <li><span class="swatch traffic-free"></span>Traffic-free</li>
            <li><span class="swatch connector"></span>Connector</li>
            <li><span class="swatch loop"></span>Your Loop</li>
            <li><span class="swatch gap"></span>Gap</li>
        </ul>
    </header>

    <figure>
        <div class="diagram-scroll">
            <img class="diagram" src="img/how-it-works.svg"
                 alt="OpenStreetMap data goes through wend network build into the network part of a PostgreSQL and PostGIS database; your Strava archive goes through wend outings import-strava into the app part. The web app loads the Segments near your start, finds and scores Loops, and shows them in your browser on a tiled map, ready to export as GPX." />
        </div>
        <figcaption>
            Two pipelines fill one database: the map from public data, your history from Strava. The planner reads
            only the part of the map near where you start. The dashed line is still to come: your runs will steer
            Loops towards ground you know, or away from it.
        </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section>
        <h2>Five jobs, three drawers</h2>
        <p>
            The database keeps the map, the place names and your history apart, so rebuilding the map from fresh
            OpenStreetMap data never touches your runs.
        </p>
        <div class="jobs">
            <article>
                <h3><code>wend network build</code></h3>
                <p>
                    Turns OpenStreetMap into Segments, the stretch of a way between two junctions. Labels each
                    one's ground, spots pavements running beside roads, and keeps only England and Wales. Joins the
                    green ground into Off-road networks and finds the car parks beside the large ones.
                </p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <h3><code>wend outings import-strava</code></h3>
                <p>
                    Reads your Strava export: runs, walks, hikes and road rides (swims and trainer rides are
                    skipped). Works out which Segments each Outing covered and where it left the map, then
                    follows each onto the map and scores it as a Loop is, for the app's stars.
                </p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <h3><code>wend places import</code></h3>
                <p>
                    Loads Ordnance Survey's place names and postcodes, so the search box can take the map anywhere
                    in England and Wales without asking any outside service.
                </p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <h3>The route finder</h3>
                <p>
                    Takes the nearby part of the map into memory and tries Loops through points round your start,
                    joining them with A*, where cost means how much you'd dislike the ground. Keeps the Loop that
                    scores best for its length.
                </p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <h3>This page's app</h3>
                <p>
                    Draws the map, takes your start and Distance, shows five Loops with their Breakdowns, and
                    hands you the GPX (or a TCX for a watch, or a QR code to send it
                    to your phone), its turns marked. Lists the car parks around the map in view, with Loop 1 from
                    each of the best five to compare. The Outings panel shows each of your runs (any you don't want deleted, for good), its gaps, and the
                    app's stars beside your own.
                </p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Words you'll see</h2>
        <dl>
            <dt>Segment</dt><dd>The stretch of one way between two junctions: the smallest piece a Loop is made of.</dd>
            <dt>Off-road</dt><dd>Unsurfaced and free of traffic: field paths, bridleways, tracks, woodland trails.</dd>
            <dt>Traffic-free</dt><dd>Paved but no cars: alleys, cut-throughs, park paths, greenwalks.</dd>
            <dt>Connector</dt><dd>Roads, and pavements beside them: the glue between the good bits.</dd>
            <dt>Outing</dt><dd>One of your own runs, walks or rides, imported from Strava or a GPX file.</dd>
            <dt>Off-road network</dt><dd>Green ground joined up, across short road links: how long a Loop can stay off roads there.</dd>
            <dt>Trailhead</dt><dd>A public car park beside a large Off-road network: the car parks listed around here.</dd>
            <dt>Gap</dt><dd>Where an Outing went more than 25 m from any way on the map: often open ground, sometimes a path OpenStreetMap is missing.</dd>
            <dt>Map gap</dt><dd>A path that stops short of another way, usually two ways that should meet in OpenStreetMap but don't, with no fence, wall, hedge, water or railway mapped between. Under 5 m it's joined and used, drawn red and dashed, and noted on the Loop card and in the GPX: check it's passable. From 5 m to 15 m it's only possible: drawn as a thin red line near the Loop, and clicked, <em>Include this time</em> takes the Loop through it.</dd>
            <dt>Stars</dt><dd>The app's, from its score (a Run's with what off-road ground and climbing add, as Loops are ranked), banded among your Outings to match how you rate: a Run's lowest tenth 1 star, its top 30% 5; and yours, given with a click. Two or more apart, and it stands out. The Outings list filters by your stars (★5, ★4–5, Not rated) and by name.</dd>
            <dt>Turn point</dt><dd>A junction where the route turns 45 degrees or more and there's a choice of way: in the GPX and the TCX, in words.</dd>
        </dl>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Keeping it up to date</h2>
        <p>
            Your Outings, stars, Saved Routes and Marks are kept apart from the map, linked by OpenStreetMap's own
            ids, so refreshing the map never loses them. Every command is in the README, with all its options.
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>To refresh</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>OpenStreetMap</td><td>Download <code>great-britain-latest.osm.pbf</code> from Geofabrik, then <code>wend network build</code> it: about 4&frac12; hours, best overnight.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Byways (Trail Rides)</td><td><code>scripts/get-rights-of-way.ps1</code>, then <code>wend network rebuild</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Place names</td><td>OS Open Names, quarterly: fetched and loaded by <strong>Update the map</strong> when OS has a newer release (the Admin panel says which is in use), or <code>wend places import</code> by hand.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Heights</td><td>OS Terrain 50, yearly: fetched by <strong>Update the map</strong> when OS has a newer release, before it builds, or new tiles in <code>data/terrain50/asc</code> and <code>wend network rebuild</code> by hand.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Your runs</td><td>A new Strava export: <code>wend outings import-strava</code> adds only what's new.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Your own data</td><td>Backed up by itself to "Wend Your Way Backups" in OneDrive, after every import and build and each day the app starts; 10 kept, spread over a month. The Admin panel backs up at a click, lists the last ten with their Outings, and puts one back, asking first.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>What it's built on</h2>
        <div class="table-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>C# on .NET 10</td><td>The app, the command line and the tests</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Blazor Server</td><td>This web page, written in C#</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>PostgreSQL 17 + PostGIS 3.6</td><td>The database that understands maps</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>osm2pgsql 2.3</td><td>Loads OpenStreetMap files into it</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Npgsql and DbUp</td><td>Talks to the database; applies its changes in order</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>OpenStreetMap via Geofabrik</td><td>The paths and roads</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Garmin FIT SDK</td><td>Reads watch files in your Strava export</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Leaflet 1.9 + Thunderforest</td><td>Draws the map; the map pictures, with Ordnance Survey, CyclOSM, OpenTopoMap, Esri's aerial photos, Waymarked Trails and access land from Natural England and Natural Resources Wales (the only online services)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>OS Terrain 50 and OS Open Names</td><td>Heights for climbing; place names and postcodes for search</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Our own A* route finder</td><td>Finds Loops, with costs that change with every Request</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>QRCoder</td><td>Draws the QR code that sends a route to your phone</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>The map pictures</h2>
        <p>
            The tiles are only the picture under the Loop: routing, scoring and GPX never use them. Choose a map with
            the switcher at the top right of the planner: Thunderforest Outdoors and OpenCycleMap (with a Thunderforest
            key), Ordnance Survey's Outdoor style (with an OS key), CyclOSM for tracks and surfaces, OpenTopoMap for
            contours, or aerial photos (Esri) to see whether a path is a real track. Signed trails (Waymarked Trails) draws
            the waymarked walking, cycling and mountain-bike routes over any of them (rest the pointer on one for its name, code and
            links to look it up), and Open access land (Natural England, Natural Resources Wales) washes
            access land, where you may go off the paths: England's from zoom 11 in, Wales's from 12. On OS's Premium plan, OS paths draws
            Ordnance Survey's paths in magenta from zoom 17 in: where one has no Loop line beside it, OpenStreetMap may lack it, and
            adding it there lets Loops use it after the next map update. Gaps in OSM marks, from zoom 14 in, where a path stops
            short of another way with nothing mapped across (yellow under 5 m, which Loops cross; grey to 15 m): click one for links
            to the two ways and OpenStreetMap's editor there, to see what the problem is. The key at the bottom right says what each colour and
            mark on screen means, only for what's on (Signed trails by level: international, national, regional, local); an
            overlay whose service is down or doesn't answer within 30 seconds says "couldn't load just now" there, rather than
            looking empty, and is asked again as the map moves; click Key to fold it away. Also on the Premium plan, OS Explorer is the paper
            Explorer map's 1:25 000 at zooms 14 to 16, best at 14 and 15, for rights of way, field boundaries and access land.
            Ordnance Survey serves it on Britain's own grid, not the web grid every other map is cut on, so while it's chosen
            the map works in that grid: the Loop, pins and clicks stay where they were, but Off-road networks, Signed trails
            and OS paths, cut on the web grid, aren't drawn over it, and come back with any other map. Your choices are remembered in this browser.
        </p>
    </section>
</main>
